chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 14 of the 3rd edition, Excel Spreadsheets, teaches the third-party module openpyxl: opening a workbook with load_workbook, reading sheets by name and the active sheet, reading cells by name and with cell(row, column), max_row and max_column, column letters and numbers, slices and the rows and columns attributes, then Project 9 (county totals from a census workbook) and Project 10 (correcting prices in a produce workbook); creating, renaming, adding and deleting sheets, writing cell values and saving; Font objects; formulas as strings and data_only; row height, column width, merging cells and freeze panes; and bar charts from a Reference and a Series, closing with 18 practice questions and 2 practice programs, a multiplication table and a blank row inserter." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's printed outputs reproduce with openpyxl 3.1.5 under Python 3.14.4 when the book's spreadsheets are replaced by stand-ins holding Table 14-1: the type of the workbook, the sheet names, the active sheet, the repr of a cell, the datetime of A1, the row, column and coordinate of B1, the loop over every other row, max_row 7 and max_column 3, the column letters 'A', 'B', 'AA' and 'AHP' and the numbers 1 and 27, the tuple of three tuples of cells for A1 to C3, the seven values of column B, the sheet names after create_sheet and del, the merged and unmerged ranges, and the freeze_panes settings of Table 14-3; both projects ran as printed on stand-in data, with the dictionary written by pprint and imported back." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's statement that an unspecified font name or size falls back to the openpyxl defaults of Calibri and 11 does not hold for a Font object: a new cell's font is Calibri 11, but Font(size=24, italic=True) has no name, Font(name='Times New Roman', bold=True) has no size, and the styles part of the saved file lists for the first only italic and a size of 24 and no name; what Excel shows for the missing attributes was not tested because Excel was not available. A Font cannot be changed after creation - setting bold on one raises AttributeError - so a different style means a new Font assigned to the cell." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's account of Reference is wrong in two places: it says the function takes five arguments and lists three, and it says the first integer of a cell position is the row, whereas the documented signature is Reference(worksheet, min_col, min_row, max_col, max_row) and Reference(sheet, 2, 3, 4, 5) printed as the range B3 to D5 of the sheet, columns first. The chapter's own example, Reference(sheet, 1, 1, 1, 10), is column 1 and rows 1 to 10, so it runs as printed, and the chart part written for it names the range A1 to A10." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P08 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Three practice questions ask for more than the chapter's text shows or for something slightly different: question 10 asks for the string name of row 14, but rows are numbered and the function the chapter teaches that returns a letter name is get_column_letter, which gives 'N' for 14; question 16 asks how to hide column C, which the chapter's introduction mentions and no example shows, and column_dimensions['C'].hidden = True was saved and read back as True; question 18 asks for five functions and methods of a bar chart, which are Reference, Series, BarChart, append and add_chart. The chapter's price list in Project 10 also says lemons while its dictionary key is 'Lemon', so a key of 'Lemons' would match nothing." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P09 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's explanation of formulas holds and needs two additions: openpyxl stores a formula as text without a value, so a file it has written and no spreadsheet application has opened gives None for that cell under data_only=True, as the run showed for the chapter's =SUM(A1:A2) and for the stand-in produce workbook after its prices were corrected; and any text that starts with an equal sign is taken for a formula, including the text =not a formula(, unless the data type of the cell is set to string. The chapter's 500 appears only after Excel has saved the file and was not reproduced." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P05 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Reading a cell creates it: on a new sheet, reading A1 and C5 left two cells and max_row 5 and max_column 3, and reading only the value of A100 made max_row 100, which the tutorial warns about; the chapter's loops of range(2, sheet.max_row + 1) depend on max_row, so code that touches a far cell first makes them run over empty rows. For large files the documentation adds a read-only mode (lazy, cells of another type, no iter_cols, must be closed) and a write-only mode (no sheets at the start, rows added only with append, saved once), which the run confirmed by the errors they raise." ; sen0414:cites chx:P03, chx:P04, chx:P09 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Current practice adds to the chapter: iter_rows and values with values_only=True for plain values, insert_rows and delete_rows, which move cells but leave a formula's text as it was (=SUM(B1:B4) moved from C5 to C7 unchanged, while Translator from openpyxl.formula.translate gave =SUM(B3:B6) for that move), copy_worksheet, dates stored as numbers in the 1900 or 1904 date system with the 1900 leap-year error and no timezones (a timezone-aware datetime is accepted on assignment and fails when the file is saved), and the refusal of old .xls files and of files that are not zip archives." ; sen0414:cites chx:P03, chx:P05, chx:P06, chx:P09 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The library's own status matters to a course: the PyPI record lists 3.1.5, uploaded 2024-06-28, as the newest release, requiring Python 3.8 or later and naming Python versions up to 3.11 in its classifiers, and also lists a beta 3.2.0b1 uploaded in 2022; the documentation the chapter points to was headed 3.1.3. The operations executed ran without a warning under Python 3.14.4 with warnings turned into errors, apart from the sheet-title warning the script provoked. The documentation's home page also says openpyxl does not guard by default against quadratic blowup or billion laughs XML attacks and advises defusedxml, which matters because an .xlsx file is a zip archive of XML." ; sen0414:cites chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P12 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter writes the county totals with pprint.pformat as the text of a Python module and imports it: pformat sorts dictionary keys by default, so the printed order is alphabetical, the generated file parses and imports back to the same dictionary (the Anchorage entry printed as {'pop': 9020, 'tracts': 2} for the stand-in rows, one population given as text and converted with int), and reading the data back by import executes the statements of the file, so such a file is to be trusted like a program." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P15, chx:P17 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 14 is best taught through what an .xlsx file is - a zip archive of XML parts in which a cell is a number, a string or a formula with no cached value - so that the chapter's calls read as operations on that structure: load_workbook reads it, the Workbook and Worksheet objects change it in memory, save writes it, and only a spreadsheet application fills in formula results. The chapter's outputs hold with openpyxl 3.1.5; the font default, the Reference arguments, reading creating cells, data_only giving None, formulas that do not move, dates, optimised modes and XML safety are what the course adds, and every statement about Excel itself remains unverified here." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P12, chx:P13 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "22 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "19 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-01T12:00:00; 13 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
